---
import Base from '@layouts/Base.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is this like Ethereum smart contracts?',
    a: 'Same goal — programmable rules on-chain — different shape. Ethereum runs programs against big shared account balances. Kaspa covenants attach rules directly to specific coins (UTXOs): smaller and stricter per contract, but the rules travel with the money itself and inherit Kaspa\'s speed and proof-of-work security.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to understand covenants to hold KAS?',
    a: 'No. Plain sending and receiving is unchanged. The stack matters when you want more than transfer: vaults, escrow, tokens with chain-enforced rules — or when you want to judge a project that claims to use them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is actually audited?',
    a: 'As of this site\'s last review: Silverscript hit v1.0.0 on 2026-09-09 but names no external audit and its own security policy still calls it experimental. Argent has no tagged release at all. The status board tracks this daily — and any project\'s "audited" claim deserves a named auditor and a date before you believe it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is KCC-20, in one line?',
    a: 'Tokens whose supply and transfer rules are checked by Kaspa consensus itself — no off-chain bookkeeper to trust. They are live and traded on mainnet while the written standard is still being consolidated.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are vProgs, and can I use them?',
    a: 'Not yet — vProgs (native programs living in the DAG) are the research track, the roadmap beyond covenants. Anyone selling you a "vProg" today is early by definition.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where would I actually start building?',
    a: 'Kascov.io lets you try covenant code with zero installation, and Silverscript is the language most things are written in today. A full build guide with tested commands is this site\'s next section to ship.',
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="FAQ — KasStacker"
  description="Straight answers to the questions everyone asks about Kaspa covenants, Silverscript, KCC-20 and what is actually audited."
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Learn · stop 5 of 5</p>
      <h1>Questions everyone asks</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="faqs">
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="finish panel">
      <h2>That's the tour.</h2>
      <p>
        You now know more about Kaspa's programmability stack than most of crypto Twitter.
        From here: the <a href="/compare">comparison matrix</a> for the details, the
        <a href="/stack">component pages</a> for depth, or the <a href="/status">status board</a>
        to see what moved today.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head { padding: 56px 0 24px; }
  .head h1 { font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 8px; }
  .faqs { display: grid; gap: 10px; }
  details {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0 20px;
  }
  details[open] { border-color: var(--teal-dim); }
  summary {
    font-family: 'Sora', sans-serif;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.97rem;
    padding: 16px 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    position: relative;
    padding-right: 28px;
  }
  summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  summary::after {
    content: '+';
    position: absolute;
    right: 2px;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    color: var(--teal);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  details[open] summary::after { content: '–'; }
  details p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.94rem; margin: 0 0 18px; max-width: 62ch; }
  .finish { margin-top: 36px; }
  .finish h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .finish p { color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }
</style>
